; ADR 0020 AT AN EXPORTED BOUNDARY: the same computation, twice, differing only
; in where the workspace comes from.
;
; ADR 0018 predicted exactly one residual gap — *"a portable program still
; allocates where a caller-supplied buffer would not, at an EXPORTED boundary"* —
; and arrays-revisited measured it at 1.07x–1.66x on Karatsuba, 10 allocations
; and 504 KB per multiply. That was measured on HAND-WRITTEN Go, because a buffer
; was not a nameable type and the Oroboros side could not be written at all.
;
; It can now. `mac-into` takes its workspace as a `(buffer int)` parameter;
; `mac-fresh` is the same body with the workspace built inside. Both are
; exported, so reduction cannot remove either boundary — which is the whole
; point, since inside a program reuse was already free.
;
; THE WORK IS PROPORTIONAL TO THE WORKSPACE, deliberately. arrays-revisited found
; the cost "growing with the size of the workspace relative to the work", so a
; kernel whose work is one pass over the buffer is where the allocation is
; comparable to the arithmetic and the difference is visible rather than lost.


(export mac-into mac-fresh)

(def n (fn () 65536))

; THE SHARED BODY. Reduction inlines it into both entry points, so the two
; measured functions differ in their signature and in nothing else — which is
; what makes the comparison a measurement of the boundary rather than of two
; programs.
; THE ELEMENT RANGES ARE DECLARED, and bounded-by-default is why: a product of
; two unbounded ints cannot be proven inside the portable window, so the program
; is refused until it says what a source element is. A byte times a byte is under
; 65536, which is what makes the store provable and what earns `w` a narrow
; element type on the hosts that have one.
(def mac (fn (w a b)
  (loop ((w w) (i 0))
    (>= i (n))  w
    else (again (set w i (* (a i) (b i))) (+ i 1)))))

; ADR 0020. The caller owns the workspace and hands it over; this never
; allocates. `w` is UNIQUE on the way in — assumed, because the caller is outside
; the program — and LINEAR through the body, which `CheckLinear` verifies from
; this signature.
; THE WORKSPACE IS DECLARED AT THE WIDTH THE OTHER SIDE INFERS, and keeping the
; two in step is the whole discipline of this comparison. `mac-fresh` BUILDS its
; workspace, so the compiler reads the element range off the stores -- a product
; of two bytes is 0..65025 -- and gives it `[]uint16`. `mac-into` is HANDED its
; workspace, so the range can only come from the declaration. Leave them
; different and the two forms differ in the SIZE of the allocation as well as in
; whether there is one, and a comparison that changes two things measures
; neither.
;
; It has now been wrong in both directions, which is why this is written out.
; First `(buffer int)` against an inferred `[]uint16`; then both plain `int`,
; because the element inference could not see through a product at all; then
; freq-2026-09-08 taught it arithmetic and the fresh side narrowed on its own
; again. The declaration follows the inference rather than fighting it.
(sig mac-into ((w (buffer (int 0 65535)))
               (a (array (int 0 255))) (b (array (int 0 255))))
              (buffer (int 0 65535))
  (where (and (<= 65536 (len w)) (<= 65536 (len a)) (<= 65536 (len b)))))
(def mac-into (fn (w a b) (mac w a b)))

; The same body, allocating its own workspace every call — what an exported
; function had to do before a buffer could be named.
(sig mac-fresh ((a (array (int 0 255))) (b (array (int 0 255)))) (array (int 0 65535))
  (where (and (<= 65536 (len a)) (<= 65536 (len b)))))
(def mac-fresh (fn (a b) (build (n) (fn (w) (mac w a b)))))
